window.UNI=[{"id":"u1","name":"Read the question first","applies":"rw","oneline":"Read the question before the passage, and you will read the passage like a hunter, not like a tourist.","story":"Before I go to the market, I read my shopping list. If I forget the list, I walk past every stall, look at everything, and still come home without the salt. With the list, my eyes ignore the shoes and the phones and look only for tomatoes, onions and salt. A passage in Reading and Writing is a crowded market. The question is your list. When you know you are hunting for the main idea, or for what one sentence does, your eyes stop wandering and start looking. I teach my students to read the list first, every time.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["In Reading and Writing, every question has a short passage and a question under it. Most students read the passage first, because that feels natural. Then they meet the question and have to go back and read again.","The code is simple: read the question first (the line that starts with 'Which choice...' or 'As used in the text...'), then read the passage. The question tells you what to hunt for. The passage is just the place where the answer lives."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Jump your eyes to the question at the bottom. Read only the question, not the options yet.","2. Say to yourself in plain words what you are hunting for. Examples: 'the main idea', 'what one named sentence does', 'which words show Text 2 disagrees', 'which fact from the table proves the claim'.","3. Read the passage with that hunt in mind. Underline or highlight the parts that match your hunt.","4. Stop reading when you have found it. Then look at the options."]},{"h":"What to hunt for, by question type","p":["Main idea question: hunt for the author's conclusion. It is often after words like 'however', 'but', 'the researchers conclude'.","Function or structure question: find the named sentence first, then read the sentence before it.","Two-text question: read Text 1 and mark its claim, then read Text 2 hunting for agreement or disagreement with that claim.","Evidence question: hunt for the exact number, quote or fact that touches the claim."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Take any five passages from the bank. For each one, cover the passage, read only the question, and write on paper a three-word hunt, for example 'author's conclusion' or 'Text 2 versus claim'. Then read the passage and see if the hunt was enough. It takes about two minutes and the habit sticks fast."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use it on every Reading and Writing question that has a passage and a question line: central ideas, text structure, cross-text, command of evidence, inferences and rhetorical synthesis.","Very short grammar questions (where the whole question is 'Which choice completes the text...') are different. There the question line is always the same, so reading it tells you nothing new. Go straight to the sentence and work from the gap."]}],"steps":["Look at the question line first. Do not read the passage yet.","Say in plain words what you are hunting for.","Read the passage once, looking only for that thing, and mark it.","Stop when you have found it, then read the options."],"why":"The Digital SAT asks a small set of question types again and again, and each type needs a different part of the passage. A main-idea question needs the conclusion, a function question needs one sentence and its neighbor, and a cross-text question needs one claim from each text. Because the question is printed under the passage, you lose nothing by reading it first. You only change where your eyes go, and a reader with a target reads faster than a reader with no target.","watch":"Do not read the question and then skip the passage. You still need to read carefully, the code just tells you where to look. On short grammar questions the question line is always the same, so this code adds nothing there.","apply":[{"skill":"ci","q":5,"without":"Read all six sentences about economists, phones, agents and districts with no target. Reach the question 'main idea'. Then test each of the four options against the passage again. Two full passes.","with":"The question says 'main idea', so I hunt for the author's conclusion. I read and look for the turn word 'however' and the words 'The researchers conclude': 'access to agents may matter more than the price of phones'. That is my target. I look at the options and find 'A survey suggests that access to agents may influence mobile-money use more than phone prices do.' The others fail quickly: 'cheaper phones reduce the use' is the opposite, 'cannot grow in districts with few agents' is too extreme (the text says use rose slightly), and 'economists are usually wrong' is too extreme.","result":"One reading with a target, then a match. Three options removed in a few seconds, no second full pass."},{"skill":"ctc","q":8,"without":"Read Text 1, read Text 2, and only then learn that the question asks which aspect of Text 1 is challenged. Go back and read both texts again looking for the clash.","with":"The question asks which aspect of Text 1 Text 2 challenges. In Text 1 I mark the claims: wildebeest sense distant rain, and they travel towards the storms. In Text 2 I hunt for the clash: herds go to places where rain fell days earlier, not where rain is falling. That hits 'sense distant rain and move towards it'. The other options fail: Text 2 still links rain to the migration, it never discusses large herds, and GPS collars are Text 2's own tool.","result":"Two short reads, each with a job. The answer, 'wildebeest can sense distant rain and move towards it', is found without a second pass."},{"skill":"tsp","q":8,"without":"Read the whole passage for meaning, then see that the question is about the sentence beginning 'Conversely', and look back to find it.","with":"The question names one sentence, so I find 'Conversely' first and read the sentence before it. Before: in some regions with many presses but few schools, few people learned to read. 'Conversely': in some areas with few presses, church-run schools produced many readers. Two observations pointing the same way, both against the idea that presses alone explain literacy. That matches 'It supplies a second observation that, with the previous one, weakens the idea that presses alone explain literacy.' 'Restates the first sentence' is wrong because the passage opposes the first sentence, and 'defines literacy' never happens.","result":"I read two sentences carefully instead of the whole passage twice, and went straight to the correct option."},{"skill":"inf","q":8,"without":"Read about pottery, clay and kilns, then reach the question and wonder what to conclude. Test all four hypotheses against the whole passage.","with":"The question asks what the missing kilns support, so I hunt for where the pots were fired. The clay is found only about 80 kilometers inland and not near the coast, and there are no kilns at the coast. If there were no ovens at the coast, the pots were not fired there. That leads to 'the pots were made and fired inland and then carried to the coast'. 'Raw clay was carried to the coast and fired there' needs a kiln at the coast, so it is the opposite. 'The coastal people did not use pottery' is wrong because they had pots. 'The inland site had no kilns' is not stated.","result":"One clear target (where were they fired?) removed three options at once."},{"skill":"ce","q":6,"without":"Study the table with all six numbers, then read the claim, then try to decide which sentence 'sounds' like it supports the claim. Often a student compares the wrong numbers.","with":"The question says the claim is that Farm A's yield increased by much more than Farm B's. So I hunt for the change in each farm, not for the highest number. Farm A: 2.0 to 3.1, up 1.1. Farm B: 2.6 to 2.7, up 0.1. The option that says 'Farm A's yield rose by 1.1 tonnes per hectare, while Farm B's rose by only 0.1' is exactly that. 'In 2024 Farm A was higher' compares levels, not growth. 'In 2020 Farm B was higher' points the wrong way. 'Both farms higher in every year' is false, because Farm B stayed at 2.6 from 2020 to 2022.","result":"I knew I needed two differences before looking at any option. Answer found in 3 steps."},{"skill":"rs","q":7,"without":"Read all four notes and then try to build a sentence in your head about the library, including the van and the town size.","with":"The goal says: show how much book borrowing grew between the first year and 2022. So I hunt for two borrowing numbers: about 1,500 in the first year and about 9,000 in 2022. Only one option has both: 'lent about 1,500 books in its first year and about 9,000 in 2022'. The mobile van (2020) and the town population (8,000) are notes about other things, so every option built around them is off target.","result":"Two numbers to find, one option contains both. Three options removed because they are missing one number or add the wrong one."}],"experiment":"Take 10 Reading and Writing questions that have passages. Do five the way you normally do, passage first, and five question first. Use a stopwatch on your phone and write the time and whether you were right for each one. After the ten, compare the average time and the number correct for the two groups. Ten questions is a small test, so do it again with ten more on another day before you decide. If question-first is not faster or better for you, keep your own way. I only want what works for you."},{"id":"u2","name":"Predict before you peek","applies":"all","oneline":"Cover the options, find your own answer first, and the wrong options lose their power to trick you.","story":"When my friend asks me to guess the price of something in a shop, I first think: 'Around 5,000.' Then I look at the tag. If the tag says 5,200, I am happy. If someone first shows me the tag and a few other prices, I forget my own idea and start to believe whatever number I see last. The SAT options are those shop tags. Some are made to look good even when they are wrong. If you decide your own answer before you look, the options cannot move your mind. You only check which one matches.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Every multiple-choice question has one right answer and three wrong ones that were written to look attractive. If you read the options first, those attractive wrong options enter your head and compete with your own thinking.","Predict-first means: cover the options with your hand (or ignore them), work out the answer yourself, then look at the options and pick the one that matches."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Read the question and the passage or the problem. Do not look at the options.","2. In Math, solve the problem completely. In Reading and Writing, say your answer in your own words.","3. Look at the options and find the one that matches your answer.","4. If nothing matches, do not panic. Re-check your work once. If your answer is still different, only then test the options one by one."]},{"h":"What prediction looks like in each part","p":["Math: solve it fully, as if there were no options. Write the number.","Words in Context: put your own word in the gap. Guess a simple word like 'praised' or 'strange'.","Transitions: decide the relationship first (same direction, opposite, cause and result, example). Say 'but' or 'so' or 'for example'.","Boundaries: say what the first part is. Is it a complete sentence or a half sentence? Then say which mark you expect.","Central ideas, inferences, evidence: say the main point or the result in one short sentence."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Choose three questions. Put a sheet of paper over the options. Write your own answer on the paper. Then move the paper and compare. Count how many times your own answer was on the list. Many students are surprised at how often it is."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use it on almost every question. It is strongest on Math questions with traps (percent, rate, signs) and on Words in Context, Transitions and Boundaries where the answer is a small piece.","It is weakest on a few questions where the options are long sentences and your prediction cannot be exact, such as some Rhetorical Synthesis questions. There, predict the content you need (for example 'both the 1,500 and the 9,000') rather than whole words."]}],"steps":["Cover the options and read only the question.","Solve it (Math) or say your own answer, word, idea or rule (Reading and Writing).","Look at the options and pick the one that matches your answer.","If no option matches, re-check once, and only then test the options."],"why":"The Digital SAT multiple-choice questions use wrong options written to be tempting: a sign error, a half-finished calculation, a word that is close in meaning. Those options only have power when you see them before you have decided. When you predict first, you are using the same skill the test is built to measure, and you use the options only as a check. It also saves time on questions where you would otherwise test all four options.","watch":"If your prediction is wrong and it matches a trap option, you will feel sure and be wrong. That is why step 4 says to re-check once when it feels too easy on a hard question. Also, on a few questions, a prediction of exact words is impossible, so predict the idea, not the words.","apply":[{"skill":"wic","q":3,"without":"Read the sentence and the four options at once: condemned, overlooked, championed, imitated. All are real words for what critics do, so each one needs a try. Up to four tests.","with":"I cover the options. 'Although the novel received harsh reviews... later critics have ______ it, calling it one of the finest works of its decade.' 'Although' says the later opinion is the opposite of the harsh one. My word: 'praised'. Now I look. 'Championed' means supported strongly, so it matches. 'Condemned' goes the same way as the harsh reviews, 'overlooked' does not fit 'calling it one of the finest', and 'imitated' is not what critics do with words.","result":"One prediction, one match, three options removed because they did not match. No testing of all four."},{"skill":"tr","q":6,"without":"Look at the options: Accordingly, Similarly, In reality, For example. Try each one in the gap and re-read the passage four times.","with":"I cover the options. She expected the harvest to double, the harvest rose only 10 percent. These two ideas clash, so my prediction is a contrast word like 'but' or 'in fact'. 'In reality' matches. 'Accordingly' needs a result, 'Similarly' needs a match, and 'For example' needs an example.","result":"The relationship is found in one read and the answer in 2 steps."},{"skill":"bd","q":3,"without":"Read 'years retired / years, retired / years; retired / years: retired' and try the sentence out loud four times. Semicolons and colons start to sound smart even when wrong.","with":"I cover the options. 'The teacher, who had taught chemistry for twenty years ______ retired in June.' The comma after 'teacher' opened an extra phrase ('who had taught chemistry for twenty years'). Extra phrases that start with a comma end with a comma. My prediction is a comma. I look: 'years, retired' is the only option with a comma. A semicolon needs a full sentence before it, and so does a colon, and 'The teacher, who had taught chemistry for twenty years' is not a full sentence.","result":"One rule, one match. Three options removed by the rule, not by feel."},{"skill":"le1","q":3,"without":"See the options 11, 13, −13, 5 and substitute each into (2x + 1)/3 = x − 4. Up to four substitutions.","with":"I cover the options and solve. (2x + 1)/3 = x − 4. Multiply both sides by 3: 2x + 1 = 3x − 12. Then 13 = x. I look: 13 is there. Check: (27)/3 = 9 and 13 − 4 = 9. The option −13 was the sign trap for students who move the numbers wrongly, and it does not pull me because I already know 13.","result":"Three algebra steps and one check instead of up to four substitutions."},{"skill":"pc","q":4,"without":"See 2,200, 2,400, 2,420 and 2,440 and feel that 2,400 looks like 10 percent plus 10 percent of 2,000. Guess.","with":"I cover the options and solve. 10 percent of 2,000 is 200, so after year one there are 2,200 students. Year two uses the new total: 10 percent of 2,200 is 220, so 2,420. I look: 2,420 is there. The 2,400 option is the trap for adding 10 percent and 10 percent of the original number, and the 2,200 option stops after year one.","result":"Two multiplications and a match. The two trap options never had a chance to look right."},{"skill":"rate","q":4,"without":"See 0.7, 17.5, 42 and 30 and decide from the look of the numbers which operation to use. Many students pick 17.5 (3.5 times 5) or 0.7.","with":"I cover the options and solve. Time equals distance divided by speed: 3.5 divided by 5 equals 0.7 hour. The question asks for minutes, so 0.7 times 60 equals 42. I look: 42 is there. The 0.7 option is the unfinished answer (still in hours), and 17.5 is the wrong operation (multiplying instead of dividing).","result":"Two steps to a final answer that already includes the unit change. The trap options were named before I looked at them."}],"experiment":"Choose 12 questions from different skills, four Math, four Words in Context or Transitions, four others. For six of them, look at the options first as usual. For the other six, cover the options and write your own answer first. Record your time and your result for each. Compare how many you got right and how long the two groups took. Also count how many times your own answer matched an option. Repeat on a second day with new questions, then decide."},{"id":"u3","name":"Every wrong answer has a defect","applies":"all","oneline":"The right answer is the only one with no defect, so find the defect in the other three and you are left with the winner.","story":"When my cousin buys a used phone at the market, he does not look for the best phone. He looks for what is wrong with each one. This one has a cracked corner. That one has a battery that dies at 60 percent. The third one sells at a good price but the charger port is loose. At the end one phone has no defect, and he buys it. The SAT is the same. Three of the four options always have a defect. Do not search for the beautiful answer. Name the defect of each wrong one.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["On the Digital SAT, each multiple-choice question has exactly one correct option. The other three are wrong for a reason. The reason is almost always one of a short list.","The code: instead of asking 'Which one is right?', ask 'What is wrong with this one?' for each option, and give the defect a name."]},{"h":"The six defects","p":["Too extreme: it uses a strong word (always, never, only, all) or goes further than the text or the facts.","Off topic: it may be true, but it does not answer the question that was asked.","Only half right: the first half is true, the second half is wrong or missing.","The opposite: it says the reverse of what the text or the numbers show.","Not stated: it sounds possible, but nothing in the text says it.","Wrong operation (Math): it comes from the wrong step, such as adding when you should multiply, or stopping before the last step."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Use the Option Eliminator in Bluebook (the tool that lets you cross out an option; you can undo it).","2. Take the options one by one. For each, find its defect and name it in two or three words.","3. Cross it out. If you cannot find a defect, leave it.","4. When one is left, check that it really has no defect, and choose it. If two are left, compare them with the question again."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Take a finished question that you answered correctly. Write the defect of each wrong option next to it. Then take one you answered wrongly and name the defect of the option you chose. This is the quickest way I know to learn how the test makes its wrong answers."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use it whenever you are not sure after your first try, and always on questions where two options look good. Naming the defect stops you from choosing the one that only feels nice.","If you already know the answer (you solved it in Math, or your prediction matches), you only need to give the other options a quick glance. Do not spend time naming every defect on a question you are sure about."]}],"steps":["Cross out options with the Option Eliminator as you go.","For each option, name its defect: too extreme, off topic, only half right, the opposite, not stated or wrong operation.","Never choose an option just because it sounds good. Choose the one with no defect.","If two are left, go back to the question and compare."],"why":"The test writers have to make three wrong options that are wrong but still tempting, and they use a small set of tricks to do it. If you know the tricks, you can spot them quickly. A named defect is also a reason you can check, while 'it feels wrong' is just a feeling. This works because the SAT has only one correct answer per question, so the proof that the others are wrong is as good as the proof that one is right.","watch":"Do not invent a defect to get rid of an option you do not like. The defect must be something you can point to in the text, the numbers or the rule. If you cannot find any defect in two options, the question needs a second look at the text, not a guess. Sometimes a true statement is the wrong answer, and 'true' is not the same as 'answers this question'.","apply":[{"skill":"ci","q":1,"without":"Read the Mariam passage, read all four options, and feel that the first is 'probably fine'. Four comparisons against the passage.","with":"Main idea of the Mariam passage: she works hard and is proud to feed her neighbors. 'Mariam dislikes waking up early': not stated. The text says the work is tiring, and she says she is proud, but never that she dislikes mornings. 'Mariam plans to leave Huye': not stated. 'Tomatoes grow best on the farm near Huye': off topic. The passage says she walks to the farm, but it does not discuss where tomatoes grow best. That leaves 'Mariam finds her hard daily work worthwhile because she feeds her neighbors', which has no defect.","result":"Three defects named, three options crossed, one left."},{"skill":"ctc","q":3,"without":"Read both texts and try to guess how Text 2's author feels, then compare with four sentences that each start 'By saying...'.","with":"Text 2 says the fungus spreads faster and kills more frogs in warmer water and that the lake has warmed. 'The fungus is not present in the lake': the opposite, because Text 2 treats the fungus as spreading there. 'The frogs have not really disappeared': the opposite, because Text 2 says the fungus kills frogs. 'The fungus is harmless to frogs': the opposite. The one left says the fungus may play a part but warming also contributes, so 'only' is too strong. No defect.","result":"Three options crossed out for the same defect (the opposite of what Text 2 says). Answer found without needing to guess the author's mood."},{"skill":"ce","q":8,"without":"Read the table and the claim and decide that 'more students at School P preferred online lessons' sounds like data. Pick the first option with numbers.","with":"The claim is that online lessons were relatively more popular at School Q. 'More students at School P (120) than at School Q (65)': wrong measure, because P simply has more students. 'At both schools, more than half preferred online': only half right. It is true (60 percent and 65 percent) but it does not compare the schools. 'Fewer students were surveyed at School Q': off topic. 'At School Q, 65 of 100 (65 percent) compared with 120 of 200 (60 percent)' compares shares, which is exactly 'relatively more popular'. No defect.","result":"Three defects named (wrong measure, only half right, off topic). Answer is the one that compares percentages."},{"skill":"pc","q":7,"without":"Try to guess: 20 up, 25 down, the answer is some mix. Try each of the four numbers by running them forward through two years of change.","with":"Revenue after year two is 54,000. Overall: 1.2 times 0.75 equals 0.9, so start times 0.9 equals 54,000, start equals 60,000. Now check the defects. 51,300 is 54,000 times 0.95: wrong operation, it treats 20 percent up and 25 percent down as a net fall of 5 percent. 72,000 is 54,000 divided by 0.75: wrong operation, it undoes only the second year. 81,000 is too big, because it would mean the business ended at two-thirds of its start, while the net change is a fall of 10 percent. 60,000 has no defect.","result":"Two options are named as wrong operations. One is named as impossible by its size. The fourth is confirmed by 60,000 times 1.2 times 0.75 equals 54,000."},{"skill":"le1","q":6,"without":"Plug each of 5, −5, 9 and 4 for k and try to decide when the equation has 'no solution'. Several long steps.","with":"Expand: k(x − 2) + 4x = 9x − 1 becomes (k + 4)x − 2k = 9x − 1. No solution happens when the x parts are equal (k + 4 = 9) but the numbers are different. So k = 5, and then −2k = −10 is not −1, so there is no solution. The defects of the others: −5 is a sign error, because then k + 4 is −1, not 9, so the x terms do not match and there is one solution. 9 and 4 are numbers copied from the equation: with k = 9 the x part is 13x on the left against 9x, and with k = 4 it is 8x against 9x, so each gives one solution, not none.","result":"The correct option is confirmed and each of the other three has a named defect (sign error, copied number, copied number)."}],"experiment":"Do 10 questions where you are normally unsure. For each, before you choose, write one defect word next to each wrong option (extreme, off topic, half, opposite, not stated, operation). After the test, count how many times you could name all three defects. Compare your result on these 10 with 10 similar questions where you just chose by feel. Look at accuracy and at time, and note the questions where you could not name a defect. Those are the ones to study."},{"id":"u4","name":"Stay inside the text","applies":"rw","oneline":"The right answer is only what the text proves, so leave your outside knowledge and your feelings at the door.","story":"In a football match, the referee cannot give a penalty because he thinks the defender is a rough player. He gives it only for what he saw on the pitch. If he used what the crowd says, or what he heard about a player last year, nobody would accept his decision. The Reading and Writing section works like this referee. You are not asked what is true in the world or what sounds wise. You are asked what this text proves. Anything that needs your own knowledge to be true is not the answer, even when it sounds very true.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Every Reading and Writing question with a passage can be answered from the passage alone. The SAT does not test whether you know the facts in the passage. It tests whether you can read carefully.","The code: for every option, you must be able to point your finger at the line in the text that proves it. If you cannot point, the option is out, even if it sounds true."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Read the question and find the claim, quote or result you need (see 'Read the question first').","2. For each option, ask: 'Which words in the text prove this?'","3. If you can point to the words and they say the same thing, keep it.","4. If you need to say 'well, in real life...' or 'it makes sense that...', cross it out. That is outside knowledge or a feeling."]},{"h":"Three kinds of outside answers","p":["The sounds-true answer: it is a nice general idea (hard work pays, sleep is important) that the text does not say.","The common-sense answer: you add a reason of your own, such as a cause that is not in the passage.","The too-far answer: the text gives a little, the option jumps much further."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Take a passage and its right answer. Underline the words in the text that prove the answer. Then take one wrong option and try to underline the proof. When you cannot, you have seen what a missing proof feels like. Do this on five questions."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use it on Inferences, Command of Evidence, Cross-Text, Central Ideas and Text Structure. It matters most where the options sound reasonable.","It does not mean ignoring vocabulary and grammar knowledge. In Words in Context and Boundaries you use language knowledge, because that is what is tested. The code is about facts and ideas, not about the meaning of words."]}],"steps":["Find in the text the exact words that answer the question.","For each option, point to its proof in the text.","Cross out every option that needs outside knowledge, a guess or 'it sounds true'.","Choose the option whose proof you can show in one line."],"why":"The Digital SAT is designed so that each Reading and Writing question has one answer that follows from the passage. Students from every country and school are tested together, and nobody has an advantage from knowing a fact the passage does not give. That means the wrong options must be wrong on the passage, not on the world. A statement that is true but not supported is the most common kind of tempting wrong option, so asking for proof removes it.","watch":"Staying inside the text does not mean copying words. The correct option often paraphrases the text in simpler words, so look for the same meaning, not the same words. Also, an inference question asks you to go one small step beyond the text, but that step must be forced by the text. If you need two steps or a fact from outside, it is too far.","apply":[{"skill":"inf","q":5,"without":"Think about why bus ridership could rise: cheaper tickets, new routes, more people moving to the city. Some of these ideas feel natural, so the student weighs all four options with real-life logic.","with":"The text says: total ridership rose 15 percent after the pass. People who rode before made the same total number of trips. Nothing else is given. 'Regular riders taking more trips': the text says their trips did not change, so it is contradicted. 'The removal of some bus routes': never mentioned, so it needs outside knowledge. 'Lower prices for single tickets': not stated. 'People who had not ridden buses before the pass': the only source left, and the text forces it.","result":"Each wrong option fails because I cannot point to its proof (or I can point to words that contradict it). The only one with proof is the new riders."},{"skill":"ctc","q":5,"without":"Think about what is true about English, French and grammar from school. 'French had a larger effect on English than any other language' sounds like a fact you may have heard, so it attracts.","with":"The question asks what both authors would agree with. Text 1 says languages change when speakers borrow words, so people who fear borrowing misunderstand language. Text 2 says English took thousands of French words after 1066 and kept its Germanic grammar. Together: borrowing words does not by itself destroy a language. For 'French had a larger effect than any other language', I cannot point to any line in either text that compares French with other languages. 'Grammar never changes in any language' has no proof (and Text 1 says languages change). 'Languages should avoid borrowing' is the opposite of Text 1.","result":"The option that sounded like general knowledge was removed because I could not point to a proof in either text."},{"skill":"ce","q":3,"without":"Think about what usually destroys a city: war, trade, disease. Choose an option because it 'sounds like what historians say'.","with":"The hypothesis is that the decline of the city was linked to a long drought. The only option that touches the drought at the right time is: tree-ring records show a severe 30-year drought that began shortly before the city was abandoned. 'A drought 500 years after the city was abandoned' cannot be a cause of the decline. 'The city's wells were deeper' says nothing about a drought. 'Many busy markets' is about trade, not drought. None of these three can point to a link between drought and decline.","result":"Only one option connects the drought and the decline in the order the hypothesis needs. Three options removed because nothing in them proves the hypothesis."},{"skill":"ci","q":8,"without":"Hear 'house sold, grandmother' and think 'she must be sad', because that is how most people feel. Choose 'Chantal feels deep grief but hides it behind a smile'.","with":"The text says she expected to feel grief. Instead she felt almost light, because the house had held complaints and quarrels. She smiled at the door. 'Deep grief but hides it': the text never says she hides anything. 'Anger': not stated. 'Regrets that the house was sold': not stated. 'Relief and lightness instead of the grief she expected' uses the text's own words.","result":"The option that sounded most like real life was wrong. The one I could prove from the text was right."},{"skill":"tsp","q":7,"without":"Read the vents passage and, from what you know about science, shape a story: 'scientists found proof, then there was debate'. Choose by your memory of how discoveries go.","with":"I list what the passage says, in order. 1) Scientists long assumed vents were too harsh for complex life. 2) In 1977 crews found dense communities of tube worms and clams, which overturned the assumption. 3) The animals use bacteria that turn chemicals into food. 4) Some researchers speculate life on other worlds might arise in similar places. That is: assumption, finding that overturned it, how it works, wider possibility. The option with those four parts is the one marked. 'Confirmed it' is wrong because the finding went against the assumption. 'Disputes how reliable the finding is' is not in the text.","result":"I built the structure from the text only, in four short steps, and then matched it. Outside memories about science were not needed."}],"experiment":"Take 10 Inference or Command of Evidence questions. For each one, before choosing, write the line number or underline the words in the passage that prove your answer. If you cannot find proof, mark the question and pick another option. Record how many answers had a proof, how many you got right, and for the ones that had no proof what the correct answer was. Compare with 10 questions you answer by feel. The difference between the two groups will show you if your feeling is too trusting."},{"id":"u5","name":"Extreme words are a warning","applies":"rw","oneline":"Words like always, never, only and all are a red flag, because the real world, and the SAT, is mostly 'often', 'may' and 'some'.","story":"When my friend says 'You NEVER call me', I only need one time I called to prove him wrong. When he says 'Sometimes you forget to call', it is hard to argue because he only needs one example. Words like never and always are easy to break. They are brave claims, and one exception ends them. The SAT uses this on purpose. A strong word in an answer option is a brave claim that the passage rarely backs up. A careful word like 'may' or 'some' is hard to break, so it is safer.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Extreme words are words that leave no room: always, never, only, completely, all, none, must, every, entirely, proves. A statement with one of these words is true only if there is no exception at all.","Passages on the SAT are usually careful. They say 'may', 'suggests', 'some', 'often', 'in many cases'. A wrong option often takes that careful idea and makes it extreme."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Circle any extreme word in an option (always, never, only, completely, all, must, every, proves, no).","2. Go back to the text and ask: does the text say it with the same strength?","3. If the text uses a careful word ('may', 'suggests', 'some') and the option uses an extreme one, cross the option out.","4. If the text itself uses the extreme word, or the facts force it, keep the option."]},{"h":"When extreme words are wrong (most of the time)","p":["The text says 'may matter more' and the option says 'always matters more'. The text gives one study and the option says 'proves'. The text talks about one group and the option says 'all'. In each case the option goes further than the evidence."]},{"h":"The rare case when they are right","p":["Sometimes the text really forces an all-or-nothing conclusion, for example when it says 'the total rose' and 'regular riders did not change', and the question says 'must have come from'. Then the strong word is earned. Also, a quoted claim in the passage can contain the extreme word, and the correct answer can be about that word, such as 'the word only is too strong'.","So extreme words are not an automatic 'no'. They are a warning light. When it turns on, check the text."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Pick five questions. Underline every extreme word in the options and in the text. For each, write 'text agrees' or 'text is more careful'. Notice how often the text is more careful."]}],"steps":["Circle extreme words in each option: always, never, only, all, completely, must, proves.","Check the text for the same strength.","Cross out the option if the text is more careful than the option.","Keep it only if the text itself forces the strong word."],"why":"A careful passage can support a careful statement, but a strong statement needs a strong proof, and a short passage rarely has one. Test writers also need wrong options that are wrong for a clear reason, and 'goes further than the text' is the clearest reason there is. This is why correct options on the SAT often use hedged words like 'may', 'suggests' and 'some'.","watch":"Do not cross out an option only because it has the word 'all' or 'only'. Check the text first. When the passage says 'all three towns' or 'only after the storm', the extreme word may be exactly right. Also, a hedge word like 'may' can appear in a wrong option, so a careful word does not make an option right by itself. Use it as a warning light only.","apply":[{"skill":"ci","q":5,"without":"Read the four main-idea options and weigh each against the passage as an equal candidate.","with":"The text says access to agents 'may matter more' than phone prices, and a survey 'suggests' it. 'Mobile-money use cannot grow in districts with few agents': 'cannot' is extreme, and the text says use rose only slightly where agents were few, so it grew. 'Economists are usually wrong about phone prices': 'usually wrong' is extreme and the text does not judge economists. The right option says 'A survey suggests ... may influence', the same careful strength as the text.","result":"Two options removed by their extreme wording alone, before any deep reading."},{"skill":"ci","q":7,"without":"Read the printing-press passage and try to decide between 'schools mattered more' and 'cheap books were unimportant' by feel.","with":"The author says literacy rose most where schools were founded, and then says 'This does not mean cheap books were unimportant. Rather... presses and schools worked together.' 'Cheap books were unimportant' is a flat claim the author denies. 'Schools mattered more to literacy than printing presses ever did' uses 'ever', a very strong word, and the author says they worked together. The correct option: 'Records from three towns suggest that cheap books alone did not raise literacy and that schools and presses worked together.' It uses 'suggest', and 'alone'.","result":"The two extreme options are both contradicted by the author's own careful sentence."},{"skill":"ctc","q":3,"without":"Read Text 1 and Text 2 and think about how Text 2 would reply in general.","with":"Text 1 uses the word 'only': the disappearance is caused only by a fungus. 'Only' is the strong word, so I check Text 2: the fungus kills more frogs in warmer water, and the lake has warmed. Text 2 does not deny the fungus, it adds a second factor. The answer is 'the fungus may play a part but warming also contributes, so only is too strong'. The wrong options are all extreme in the other direction: the fungus is 'not present', the frogs 'have not really disappeared', the fungus is 'harmless'.","result":"Here the extreme word is in the passage, and the correct answer is about it. Three options removed because they deny too much."},{"skill":"ctc","q":4,"without":"Choose between 'It proves that Text 1 is wrong for every kind of worker' and 'It confirms Text 1' by general feeling.","with":"Text 2 is a study of customer-service staff, one kind of worker. 'Proves ... wrong for every kind of worker': 'proves' and 'every kind' are both extreme, and one study of one job cannot do that. 'Confirms the claim with new data' is the opposite, because the home workers resolved more cases. The correct option says Text 2 'provides data that calls Text 1's general claim into question, at least for one type of job'. It is careful: 'calls into question', 'at least for one type'.","result":"The correct option is the one that is as careful as the evidence."},{"skill":"rs","q":8,"without":"Read Okoye's notes and the four sentences and choose the sentence that sounds like a summary.","with":"The notes say traders using mobile payments reported fewer thefts, they paid a small fee, and the fees were small compared with the losses avoided. 'Mobile payments are free of cost and prevent all theft' has 'free' and 'all', both extreme, and the notes say there is a fee. 'Mobile payments have no downsides' has 'no', again extreme, and the fees are a downside. The correct option says fewer cash thefts and small fees, 'small compared with the losses avoided'.","result":"Two options removed by extreme wording, one removed because it ignores the downside the goal asks for."},{"skill":"inf","q":5,"without":"See the stem 'must have come from' and think that is an extreme word, so be afraid of every option.","with":"This is the rare case. The strong word 'must' is in the stem, and the text earns it. Total ridership rose 15 percent, and the trips of people who rode before did not change. So the rise has to come from someone else, and the only option that says so is 'people who had not ridden buses before the pass'. The other options do not have extreme words, they just have no proof.","result":"A strong word does not always mean a wrong answer. Check whether the text forces it. Here it does."}],"experiment":"Take 12 Reading and Writing questions with long options. On a sheet of paper, write for each: how many options had an extreme word, whether the correct one had one, and whether the text was careful or strong in that spot. Count how many of the correct answers had an extreme word. Do not decide from one session. Do it again with 12 new questions. You will then know how much to trust the warning for yourself, and you will see the rare cases for yourself."},{"id":"u6","name":"Never leave a blank, guess smart","applies":"all","oneline":"A blank can never score, and a guess sometimes does, so every question gets an answer before the module ends.","story":"Think of a free raffle at a school event. The ticket costs nothing, and if your number is not called, you lose nothing. Only a student who did not take a ticket has no chance to win. The SAT counts the questions you get right and does not take marks away for a wrong answer. An empty bubble is the ticket you did not take. Take every ticket, and make it as strong as you can first.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["On the Digital SAT, your raw score is the number of questions you answer correctly. There is no penalty for a wrong answer. So a blank has a score of zero for sure, and a guess has a chance of a point.","The code has two parts. Part one: never leave a blank. Part two: when you must guess, guess smart, by removing the options you can prove wrong first."]},{"h":"The facts about Bluebook you need","p":["You can move around inside a module and use 'mark for review' (the bookmark) to come back to a question. The question menu shows which questions are answered and which are marked.","Once you leave a module, you cannot go back to it. So before time ends, open the question menu and check that every question has an answer. A question left blank in the menu is a point you gave away."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Do your best on the question for your time budget. If you do not have the answer, cross out every option with a named defect (see 'Every wrong answer has a defect').","2. Pick one of the options that are left. Enter it right now, so that it is never blank.","3. Mark the question for review with the bookmark if you want to return to it.","4. Two minutes before the module ends, open the question menu and fill every blank. Use the last seconds only to enter answers, not to start long problems."]},{"h":"How to guess smart","p":["The odds are simple. With four options and no idea, you have 1 chance in 4. If you remove one option, it is 1 in 3. If you remove two, it is 1 in 2. Every option you remove with a real reason raises your chances.","In Reading and Writing, remove options that are off topic, too extreme, the opposite or not stated. In Math, use the options: put an option into the question and check (back-solving), or use Desmos to see which one fits. If you cannot remove any, pick any option and move on. Do not waste a minute choosing among four equal options, and do not follow a pattern of letters, because the test is not made with a letter pattern."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Practice in a mock test: when time is nearly up, answer the last questions with a smart guess instead of leaving them. Write on paper which options you removed and why. Later, check how many smart guesses were right."]}],"steps":["Eliminate every option you can prove wrong.","Enter an answer at once from what is left, so there is no blank.","Mark the question for review if you want to return.","Before the module ends, open the question menu and fill every blank."],"why":"College Board scores the Digital SAT by counting correct answers, with no deduction for wrong ones, and this is widely published. That means a guess can only help. Because you cannot return to a module after you leave it, a blank at that moment is permanent. Removing options before you guess is not a trick, it is simply using what you know, and it raises the chance of a point from 1 in 4 to 1 in 3 or 1 in 2.","watch":"Guessing is a safety net, not a plan. If you guess on every hard question without trying, you will lose points you could have earned. The code also does not tell you to rush every question. It tells you what to do when time or knowledge has run out. And please do not trust any pattern like 'C is more common'. I cannot show you that such a pattern helps.","apply":[{"skill":"wic","q":8,"without":"With 25 seconds left, you do not know 'illusory', so you leave it blank, or you pick quickly without looking.","with":"The sentence says the 'apparent recovery' of the kelp forest may be ______, and that the roots remain badly weakened. I do not need to know every word. 'Substantial' means large and real, and the text says weakened, so it is the opposite. 'Unprecedented' means never seen before, and nothing is said about that, so it is not stated. Two left: 'illusory' and 'gradual'. 'Apparent' means it looks like recovery. 'Gradual' says slow, but the text says a storm could undo it, which is about weakness, not speed. I choose 'illusory' and mark it for review.","result":"From 1 chance in 4 to 1 in 2 in about 15 seconds, and here the reasoning leads to the marked answer, 'illusory'."},{"skill":"inf","q":7,"without":"The passage about color words is long. With time running out, leave it blank.","with":"Quickly, without reading everything: the question is what the speed of Language X speakers depends on. 'Better eyesight' is off topic, since the passage never mentions eyesight. 'A better memory for numbers' is off topic too, since the digits were only a task to occupy the mind. Two left. 'The color words being irrelevant' goes against the passage, because the color words gave an advantage until the digits task took it away. So I enter 'their use of internal verbal labels while they work on the task'.","result":"Even a student who read only the question and skimmed could remove two options and enter the correct answer."},{"skill":"tr","q":8,"without":"The two sentences about the phone feel confusing, so you close your eyes and tap an option.","with":"First sentence: the battery is great, but reviewers rated the phone poorly. Second: the screen cracked, the camera struggled. 'Consequently' says the second is a result of the poor rating, but cracked screens are the reason for the rating, so it is the wrong direction. 'Likewise' needs a similar second item, and there is none. Left: 'Nevertheless' and 'Specifically'. 'Nevertheless' needs a contrast, but the screen and camera support the poor rating. So 'Specifically', which introduces the details of the reasons.","result":"Two options removed with a reason each, a 50 percent chance at worst, and a good reason to pick the right one."},{"skill":"pc","q":8,"without":"Skip this math question because the salt-solution wording is hard, and leave it blank.","with":"I use the options. Start: 40 liters at 15 percent is 6 liters of salt. Try each: add 10 liters, salt is 6 out of 50, which is 12 percent. Add 16, 6 out of 56, about 10.7 percent. Add 20, 6 out of 60, exactly 10 percent. Add 25, 6 out of 65, about 9.2 percent. The 20 matches. Even if I only had time for two tests, I could remove the two options that are too far.","result":"Back-solving turns a hard question into four quick checks, and I never leave it blank. Option C, 20, is confirmed by the check."},{"skill":"le1","q":8,"without":"With 20 seconds left, leave the 'constant a' question blank because you cannot set up the algebra.","with":"The solution is twice a. So try an option for a. Try a = −5: the solution is x = −10. Check 3(x − a) = 3(−10 + 5) = −15, and 2x + 5 = −20 + 5 = −15. They are equal, so −5 works. If I tried a = 5 first: x = 10, 3(10 − 5) = 15 but 2(10) + 5 = 25, so no. One or two tests are enough.","result":"One check of about 10 seconds finds the answer. A blank would have been 0 for sure."}],"experiment":"Do a timed set of 20 questions (about 24 minutes for Reading and Writing or about 32 for Math). For the last 5 questions, pretend you only have 15 seconds each. Write the options you can remove and pick from what is left, and never leave a blank. Afterward, check them and count how many of those 5 were right. Repeat with another set of 20 on another day. Do not expect a promise, just compare how many guesses were right with the 1-in-4 you would get with no thinking."},{"id":"u7","name":"Run the clock: the time bank","applies":"all","oneline":"Spend little on the easy questions, save the seconds, and spend them on the hard ones where they matter.","story":"When I plan my money for the month, I do not spend the same amount every day. Some days I spend almost nothing, so on the day of a big bill I have enough saved. A student who spends money equally every day has no savings and panics when the bill comes. Time on the SAT is the same. About 71 seconds per question in Reading and Writing and 95 seconds in Math is the average. Easy questions take less, hard ones take more. Save seconds on the easy ones and spend them on the hard ones.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Reading and Writing has 54 questions in 64 minutes, so about 71 seconds per question. Math has 44 questions in 70 minutes, so about 95 seconds per question. These are averages, not rules for each question. Each section has 2 modules timed separately.","The time bank: when you answer an easy question in 20 seconds, you have saved about 50 seconds (Reading and Writing) or 75 seconds (Math). Those seconds are in the bank for the hard questions."]},{"h":"The exact steps","p":["1. Use the question menu in Bluebook to see all questions in the module. Do the easy ones first and mark the hard ones for review with the bookmark.","2. On an easy question, answer quickly. Do not double-check what you are sure about. The saved seconds are your savings.","3. If a question has taken about twice its average (about 2 minutes in Reading and Writing, about 3 minutes in Math) and you still have no plan, enter your best smart guess, mark it, and move on.","4. When the easy ones are done, go back to the marked questions and spend the saved time on them, one by one."]},{"h":"Handling the timer","p":["Bluebook has a timer that you can hide until 5 minutes remain. If watching the clock makes you nervous, hide it and use it only as a signal. If you hide it, make sure you use the 5-minute mark as the time to check that every question has an answer. If the clock helps you, leave it on."]},{"h":"What about the second module?","p":["The second module adapts to how you did in the first. It may feel harder, and that is not a sign that you are doing badly. The same plan works: easy first, bank the time, spend it on the hard ones, never leave a blank."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["On your next practice module, write on a sheet the time at the start and at the middle. At the middle, you should have answered about half the questions. Also note the number of questions you flagged. After the module, write down which ones took more than twice the average and why."]}],"steps":["Open the question menu and do the easy questions first.","Answer easy questions fast, and save the seconds.","If a question takes twice its average with no plan, guess smart, mark it, and move on.","Return to marked questions and spend the saved time.","Hide the timer until 5 minutes remain if it makes you nervous."],"why":"Every question is worth the same amount of score, whether it takes 15 seconds or 3 minutes. So the best use of a fixed amount of time is to collect the cheap points first and make sure the hard ones get the seconds that remain. Bluebook lets you move freely inside a module, with a question menu and mark for review, so you can do this in the order you choose. Once you leave a module you cannot go back, so the work must be finished inside it.","watch":"Do not assume the questions go from easy to hard. The order is not a reliable guide, so read the first few words of a question before you decide it is a skip. Also, do not rush the easy ones so much that you make careless mistakes. Easy does not mean careless. The times I give for the bank questions are my estimates for a prepared student, and your numbers will be different, so measure them yourself.","apply":[{"skill":"wic","q":1,"without":"A student reads 'The river is usually calm, but after three days of heavy rain...' and tests all four options slowly, taking about a minute and a half, checking again after choosing.","with":"This is an easy question. 'Usually calm, but after heavy rain...' so the river became the opposite of calm: wild, rough. 'Turbulent' matches. 'Peaceful', 'shallow' and 'silent' do not fit flooding. About 15 seconds.","result":"About 15 seconds against an average of 71. About 55 seconds go into the bank."},{"skill":"le1","q":1,"without":"A student tries to be careful, writes everything out, and then checks every option, about 70 seconds.","with":"3x + 7 = 22. Take away 7: 3x = 15. Divide by 3: x = 5. It matches the second option. About 15 to 20 seconds.","result":"About 20 seconds against an average of 95. About 75 seconds go into the bank."},{"skill":"ci","q":7,"without":"A hard Reading and Writing question, taking 70 seconds only because the student has no time left from the early questions. Rushed, and the wrong 'schools mattered more' tempts.","with":"This one is long, with a twist: 'Yet... this does not mean cheap books were unimportant.' I allow myself about 100 seconds because I have banked time from the easy ones. I read for the author's final point: presses and schools worked together. The correct option, 'Records from three towns suggest that cheap books alone did not raise literacy and that schools and presses worked together'.","result":"About 100 seconds, which is 30 seconds more than the average. Two easy Reading and Writing questions at 15 seconds each paid for it."},{"skill":"rate","q":8,"without":"A hard Math question with two people and different start times. The student works without a plan for 3 minutes or more, and has no time later.","with":"I allow about 150 seconds. Jean starts at 8:00 at 4 km/h. By 8:30 he is 2 km ahead. Marie rides at 12 km/h, so she gains 12 minus 4, which is 8 km every hour. To close 2 km at 8 km/h takes 0.25 hour, which is 15 minutes. 8:30 plus 15 minutes is 8:45. This matches 8:45.","result":"About 150 seconds, which is about 55 seconds above the average. The easy Math question above, 75 seconds in the bank, paid for it."},{"skill":"circ","q":8,"without":"A very hard Math question. The student stays 4 minutes trying to remember the circle formula and misses two easy questions at the end of the module.","with":"At the first read I look for a plan: this is a circle in expanded form, so complete the square. If I do not see that plan after about 45 seconds, I flag it, enter a guess, and move on. If I do see it: x² + 4x becomes (x + 2)² − 4, and y² − 10y becomes (y − 5)² − 25. So (x + 2)² + (y − 5)² = 29 − k. The radius is 6, so 36 = 29 − k, and k = −7. This matches −7. About 100 seconds when the plan is clear.","result":"Either about 100 seconds with the plan, or 45 seconds spent and a flagged guess. In both cases the module time stays under control."}],"experiment":"Do one timed module of practice questions twice, on two different days, with different questions of similar difficulty. The first time, go from question 1 to the end without any plan. The second time, use the question menu, do the easy ones first, flag the hard ones, and use the banked time on them. Write the number correct, the number of blanks and the time you had left. Compare the two. One module is a small test, so do it a few times before you judge, and note whether hiding the timer until 5 minutes remain helped or hurt you."},{"id":"u8","name":"Last sentence first","applies":"m","oneline":"Read what the question asks before you touch the numbers, and you will stop solving the wrong thing.","story":"When I go to the market, I never start by looking at everything on the tables. First I decide what I came for: tomatoes, onions, one bag of rice. Then I walk straight to the right stall and ignore the rest. A Math question is like a big market. The story at the top has many numbers, names and details, and most students pick up everything. I do it differently. I read the last sentence first, so I know exactly what I came to buy, and then I go through the story and take only the numbers I need.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Almost every Math word problem puts the real question in the last sentence. It starts with words like 'What is', 'How many', 'Which of the following' or 'For what value'. The sentences before it only give you facts.","The code is simple: read the last sentence first, underline the target, and only then read the rest. You read the same words, but you read them with a purpose."]},{"h":"Why students lose marks without it","p":["Many wrong options on the SAT are not random. They are the answers to a different question. If the problem asks for x + y, the value of x alone will be in the options. If it asks 'how many are NOT', the number who ARE will be in the options. If it asks for minutes, the answer in hours may be there.","When you only read the last sentence at the end, you are tired and in a hurry, and your eyes see a number that looks right. When you read it first, you know what you are looking for the whole time."]},{"h":"The steps","p":["1. Go straight to the last sentence. Read it slowly, once.","2. Underline the target. Underline words like x + y, 'how many more', 'NOT', 'greatest', 'in minutes', 'per week'. Use the highlight tool on the screen or draw a line on your scratch paper.","3. Say in your own words what the answer will look like: a number? a count? a unit? a point? a sum of two things?","4. Now read the beginning and collect only the facts you need.","5. Before you choose, look at the underline again."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Read only the last sentence of each problem below and say what the answer will look like. Do not solve.","a) 'What is the value of 2x - 3?' Answer: a number made from x, not x itself.","b) 'How many club members are NOT in Grade 12?' Answer: a count of the people left out.","c) 'How many more kilometers does Car A travel than Car B?' Answer: a difference of two distances.","This takes 5 seconds each. It is the whole code."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Always. It costs about five seconds and it is the cheapest habit in this book. It helps most in word problems, systems of equations, percent, rate and geometry questions, and in any problem with two or more unknowns.","It helps less in a bare question like 'What is the value of f(5)?' because the whole question is already one line. Even there, a glance at the last words is free."]}],"steps":["Jump to the last sentence and read it slowly.","Underline the target: x + y, how many more, NOT, greatest, the unit.","Say what the answer should look like before you solve.","Now read the story and take only the numbers you need.","Before you choose, look back at your underline."],"why":"The SAT writes wrong options to match the common mistakes of students who answered a different question: the single variable instead of the sum, the amount that is left in instead of left out, the wrong unit. Because each Math module gives you only about 95 seconds a question, you cannot afford to solve a full problem and then find out you solved the wrong thing. Reading the target first costs a few seconds and tells you which numbers matter. It also works for every Math question, because every Math question is built the same way: facts first, question last.","watch":"Do not skip the first part of the problem. The last sentence tells you what to find, but the facts you need are above it, and sometimes a key condition such as 'for x not equal to 3' is hidden there. Use the code to guide your reading, not to replace it.","apply":[{"skill":"le1","q":4,"without":"A student reads the taxi story from the top, sees 1,500, 600 and 6,900, and starts to calculate. Many add or multiply 1,500 + 600 or divide 6,900 by 600 (11.5) without a plan. That is 3 or 4 tries.","with":"Read the last sentence first: 'How many kilometres was the trip?' Underline 'how many kilometres'. The answer is a number of kilometres, so I need an equation with kilometres in it. Now I read the story: 1,500 to start plus 600 per kilometre equals 6,900. So 1,500 + 600k = 6,900, then 600k = 5,400, so k = 9. Option B (9) is correct.","result":"One equation, three steps. Knowing the target was 'kilometres' showed me the unknown before I started."},{"skill":"sys","q":3,"without":"A student sees 3x + 2y = 16 and x - y = 2, solves for x and for y, gets x = 4 and y = 2, and may stop at x = 4 because it is in the options as 4.","with":"Last sentence first: 'What is the value of x + y?' I underline x + y. Now I know I need a sum, not a single variable. I solve: x = y + 2, so 3(y + 2) + 2y = 16, so 5y = 10 and y = 2, then x = 4. The sum is x + y = 6. Option C (6) is correct.","result":"The trap option '4' (the value of x) is exposed before I solve, because my underline says 'sum'. I finish in the same number of steps with the right target."},{"skill":"pc","q":6,"without":"A student finds the club members (55% of 200 = 110), then 30% of them (33) and picks 33 because it is the last number they calculated.","with":"Last sentence first: 'How many club members are NOT in Grade 12?' I underline NOT. The answer is the part left over. Now I calculate: 55% of 200 = 110 club members. 30% of 110 = 33 are in Grade 12. The members NOT in Grade 12 are 110 - 33 = 77. Option C (77) is correct. The number 33, which is the trap, is in the options, but my underline stops me.","result":"One trap option avoided on purpose. The word NOT is the most important word in the problem, and underlining it takes 2 seconds."},{"skill":"rr","q":8,"without":"A student reads the speeds 72 km/h and 18 m/s, mixes the units and may calculate 72 - 18 = 54 or 72 x 2 = 144. Both numbers are in the options.","with":"Last sentence first: 'How many more kilometers than Car B does Car A travel in 2 hours?' I underline 'more kilometers' and '2 hours'. The target is a difference, in kilometers. Car A travels 72 x 2 = 144 km. Car B: 18 m/s x 3,600 = 64,800 m per hour, which is 64.8 km per hour, so 129.6 km in 2 hours. The difference is 144 - 129.6 = 14.4. Option B (14.4) is correct.","result":"The underline tells me I must put both cars in kilometers and subtract. That removes options 54 and 144 (each answers a different question) before I do any detailed work."},{"skill":"ne","q":6,"without":"A student solves the system, finds two solutions, (3, 4) and (-4, -3), and does not know which one to pick, or picks x = 3 and stops.","with":"Last sentence first: 'what is the greatest possible value of x + y?' I underline 'greatest' and 'x + y'. So I need to find both solutions and compare the sums. Put y = x + 1 into x² + y² = 25: x² + (x + 1)² = 25, so 2x² + 2x - 24 = 0, so x² + x - 12 = 0, so x = 3 or x = -4. The solutions are (3, 4) and (-4, -3). The sums are 7 and -7. The greatest is 7. Option C (7) is correct.","result":"The underline tells me to find BOTH solutions and compare their sums, so I do not stop after the first one."}],"experiment":"Take 10 Math word problems from this app. Do 5 the way you normally do, and 5 with this code. For each one, write down the time you used and whether you were right. Also count how many times you caught yourself about to answer a different question than the one asked. After the 10 questions, compare the right answers and the average time. If the code does not help you, you will see it in the numbers."},{"id":"u9","name":"Desmos first","applies":"m","oneline":"The graphing calculator inside Bluebook can do in ten seconds what takes me two minutes by hand, if I know the exact moves.","story":"When I want to know the way to a place in Kigali, I can try to remember all the roads, or I can ask a person who lives there. Both can work, but asking is quicker when the roads are confusing. Desmos is the person who lives in the math. Instead of working through every step of an equation, I type it in and look at the picture. The picture shows me the answer: where two lines meet, the top of a hill, the points where a curve crosses the ground. On the SAT I am allowed to ask.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Bluebook has a graphing calculator, called Desmos, built into every Math question. You open it from the Math screen, and you can drag it around. It is the same calculator for the whole test, so you can practise it at home for free.","The code: when a question is about a graph, a system, a parabola or an equation that is hard to solve by hand, type it into Desmos first and read the answer from the picture."]},{"h":"The five moves you must know","p":["1. Intersection of two graphs. Type the first equation on line 1, the second on line 2. Tap the dot where the graphs cross. A small box shows the coordinates. This solves systems of equations, including a line and a curve.","2. Solving an equation. Type the left side as y = (left side) and the right side as y = (right side). The x-coordinate of each crossing is a solution. For something like 2x² - 8x - 10 = 0, just type 2x^2-8x-10 and tap where the curve touches the x-axis.","3. Top or bottom of a parabola. Type the function. Tap the highest or lowest point of the curve. You get the vertex, so you know the maximum or minimum.","4. Checking values. Type f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 on line 1 and f(5) on line 2. Desmos prints the number (8). This is quick for 'what is f(5)' and for testing answer options. You can also add a table with the plus sign to see many outputs at once.","5. Regression. Press the plus sign, add a table, type the x-values and y-values, then on a new line type y1 ~ m x1 + b. Desmos gives you the best line of fit.","Do not forget the exponent symbol: type ^ for a power. If the picture is off the screen, use the minus button to zoom out until you can see the curve."]},{"h":"The sliders trick","p":["If you type an equation that has a letter that is not x or y, such as y = 2x + k, Desmos offers to make a slider for k. Then you can set k to any number and watch the graph change. This is perfect when the question says 'for what value of k', because you can test the options one by one."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Open Desmos. Type y = x^2 - 4x + 3 and tap the two points where the curve crosses the x-axis. You should see (1, 0) and (3, 0). Now type y = x - 1 on line 2 and tap the two places where the line and the curve cross. You should see (1, 0) and (4, 3). If your points do not match, check your typing.","Practise the five moves until each one takes under 15 seconds."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use Desmos first when you see: a system with a curve in it, an equation that does not factor easily, 'maximum' or 'minimum' of a parabola, a table of data with a request for the line of best fit, or 'for what value of k' with a graph.","Do not use it for a simple one-step question such as 3x + 7 = 22. Your head is faster there."]}],"steps":["Open Desmos and type the equation or equations exactly as they are written.","Use ^ for powers, and zoom out with the minus button if the graph is off the screen.","Tap the dot you need: the crossing, the x-intercept or the top of the curve.","For 'for what value of k', type k in the equation and move the slider.","Read the coordinates, find the matching option, then check the last sentence of the question once."],"why":"Digital SAT Math lets you use a built-in graphing calculator on every question, so the test writers know that graphs can be used. Many questions that look hard by hand are very short in Desmos, because the calculator solves in one tap what takes the student five steps of algebra. The calculator also lowers the chance of small arithmetic slips, and every Math question gives you about 95 seconds, so a ten-second picture is a good use of time. It is not cheating. It is a tool the College Board put in the room for you.","watch":"Desmos is slow if you type slowly, and it can mislead if the answer is not an easy decimal or the graph is hiding off the screen. If the question asks for an exact answer such as 3 + √2 or a fraction, Desmos gives you a decimal, and you must match it to the option. Also, when the algebra is two lines, do it by hand. Practise both so you can choose.","apply":[{"skill":"ne","q":4,"without":"Set x² - 3 = 2x + 5, move everything to one side: x² - 2x - 8 = 0, factor as (x - 4)(x + 2) = 0, find x = 4 or x = -2, then put x back into one equation to find y. About 4 to 5 steps.","with":"Type y = x^2 - 3 on line 1 and y = 2x + 5 on line 2. Tap the two crossing dots. Desmos shows (-2, 1) and (4, 13). Look at the options: only (4, 13) is there. Option C is correct.","result":"Two lines typed, two taps, and the point is on the screen with both coordinates. No factoring and no second substitution."},{"skill":"nf","q":5,"without":"Find the vertex by hand: t = -b/(2a) = -64/(2 x -16) = 2, then put t = 2 back: -16(4) + 64(2) + 80 = -64 + 128 + 80 = 144. Three steps, and a sign slip is easy.","with":"Type y = -16x^2 + 64x + 80 (use x for t). Zoom out with the minus button until the hill shows. Tap the top of the hill. Desmos shows (2, 144). The maximum height is 144 feet. Option C is correct.","result":"One typed line and one tap. Desmos also shows that the height at x = 0 is 80, so I can see why option A (80) is only the starting height."},{"skill":"ne","q":5,"without":"Set x² - 2x + 3 = 2x + k, move to one side, x² - 4x + (3 - k) = 0, then set the discriminant to zero: 16 - 4(3 - k) = 0, and solve for k. Needs a rule that many students forget.","with":"Type y = x^2 - 2x + 3 and y = 2x + k. Allow Desmos to make a slider for k. Set k = -1: the line touches the curve at one point only, (2, 3). Try the other options to be sure: k = 0 cuts the curve in two points (x = 1 and 3), k = 1 and k = 3 also cut it twice. Only k = -1 gives exactly one point. Option A is correct.","result":"I do not need the discriminant rule. I look at the picture and count the points: one touch point means the answer."},{"skill":"td","q":3,"without":"Work out the mean of the x-values and y-values, the sum of products, the slope and the intercept by hand, or guess from the picture of the options. Long and easy to get wrong.","with":"Open a table in Desmos. Type x1 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and y1 = 9, 8, 6, 5, 3. On a new line type y1 ~ m x1 + b. Desmos shows m = -1.5 and b = 10.7. The line is y = -1.5x + 10.7. Option B is correct. Note that the slope must be negative because the y-values fall as x grows, which already removes option A.","result":"One table and one typed line give the best-fit line, and the sign check agrees with the data."},{"skill":"lf","q":6,"without":"Set 250 + 40n = 65n, subtract 40n from both sides, 250 = 25n, divide, n = 10. Easy for strong students, but a slip with the 40n or 65n would put 6, 25 or 38 as an answer.","with":"Type y = 250 + 40x and y = 65x. Tap the crossing. Desmos shows (10, 650). So 10 bags make the income equal to the cost. Option C is correct.","result":"Two lines and one tap. As a bonus, the picture shows both the number of bags (10) and the money (650 dollars), so I can see which one the question asks for."}],"experiment":"Choose 6 problems from the Nonlinear, Systems or Linear Functions skills. Solve 3 by hand and 3 with Desmos, and write down the time for each. If you are slow in Desmos, that is normal: it comes from not knowing the moves, so repeat the five moves above until they are quick. After one week, redo the test. Compare time and mistakes."},{"id":"u10","name":"Back-solve: plug in the answers","applies":"m","oneline":"When the question gives four possible answers, I test them instead of building the answer from nothing.","story":"When my mother sends me to buy a pair of shoes for my little sister, I do not measure her feet and do the maths of sizes. I take the size 36 shoe, ask her to try it on, and see if it fits. If it is too tight, I go to 37. The shoe shop gave me the options, so I test them. On a multiple-choice Math question the SAT does the same: it gives you four numbers, one of which is right. Why not try them on, like shoes?","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Every Math question in this app has four options, and in many problems the options are numbers. The code is this: instead of solving the equation, put an option into the problem and see if it works. The option that makes the statement true is the answer.","This is called back-solving. It is not cheating. It is the same as checking your work, but you do it first."]},{"h":"The steps","p":["1. Look at the options and put them in order from smallest to biggest in your head or on your paper.","2. Start with a middle value, the second or third number. Do not start with the first.","3. Put it into the problem and check if the statement is true.","4. If it is not true, decide if the number is too big or too small. This tells you to go up or down.","5. Test the next option in that direction. You will finish within two tests, almost always.","6. If a test works, stop. Do not test the rest."]},{"h":"Why start in the middle","p":["There are four options. If you test one of the two in the middle and learn that your number is too small, you know the answer is higher. That removes the smaller options without testing them. If you start at the end, you learn almost nothing. So the middle test is worth more.","Example: options 5, 11, 13 and -13 sorted as -13, 5, 11, 13. Test 11. If the result is too low, only 13 is left."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Question: What is the solution of 2x + 6 = 20? Options: 5, 7, 9, 14. Start with 7 (a middle value). Test: 2 x 7 + 6 = 20. It works. Stop. The answer is 7.","Now do it again with x - 4 = 11, options 7, 10, 15, 21. Start with 10: 10 - 4 = 6, too low. Go up to 15: 15 - 4 = 11. It works."]},{"h":"When it helps and when it wastes time","p":["It helps when the equation is hard to solve but easy to check: equations with fractions, word problems with a total and two prices, angle problems with an unknown, and 'which value could m be' questions.","It wastes time when the algebra is only one or two steps (3x + 7 = 22 is quicker by hand), when the options are letters, expressions or graphs, and when the options are not numbers.","It also can mislead when the question says 'greatest' or 'least'. Then you must test the biggest option first for 'greatest' and make sure the bigger options fail."]}],"steps":["Check that the four options are numbers and the equation is hard to solve but easy to check.","Order the options small to big and test a middle one first.","Put it into the problem and see if it works.","If it fails, decide if you need a bigger or a smaller number and test the next option in that direction.","Stop at the first option that works, unless the question says greatest or least."],"why":"Multiple-choice questions on the SAT give exactly four options and only one is correct, so checking an option is a complete method of solving. Checking a number is usually one or two simple calculations, while solving can need five steps with signs and fractions. The test also lets you move back and forth in a module and there is no penalty for a wrong answer, so a fast check is a safe way to save time for the harder questions. Starting in the middle matters because each failed test tells you to go up or down, so you need at most two tests.","watch":"Back-solving is slower than solving when the problem has only one or two steps, and it does not work when the options are expressions or letters. Do not use it on questions that say 'which could be' without a clear way to test, and take care with 'greatest' or 'least': the first option that works may not be the one the question wants.","apply":[{"skill":"le1","q":3,"without":"Multiply both sides by 3: 2x + 1 = 3(x - 4) = 3x - 12, so 13 = x. Fractions make students slip. About 3 to 4 steps.","with":"Sort the options: -13, 5, 11, 13. Test 11. Left side: (2 x 11 + 1)/3 = 23/3, about 7.67. Right side: 11 - 4 = 7. The left is bigger, so 11 is too small (the left side shrinks as x grows). Go up to 13: left (27)/3 = 9, right 13 - 4 = 9. It works. Option B (13) is correct.","result":"Two quick tests, and no fractions to clear. The first test told me which way to go."},{"skill":"sys","q":4,"without":"Write a + c = 40 and 5,000a + 2,000c = 164,000, then substitute: 5,000a + 2,000(40 - a) = 164,000, so 3,000a = 84,000 and a = 28. Four steps with large numbers.","with":"Sort the options: 12, 20, 28, 30 adult tickets. Test 20: the other 20 are child tickets, so the money is 20 x 5,000 + 20 x 2,000 = 100,000 + 40,000 = 140,000. That is less than 164,000, so I need more adult tickets. Test 28: 28 x 5,000 + 12 x 2,000 = 140,000 + 24,000 = 164,000. It works. Option C (28) is correct.","result":"Two tests with simple multiplication instead of an equation. The first test (too little money) told me to go up."},{"skill":"lat","q":3,"without":"Alternate interior angles are equal, so 3x + 10 = 5x - 30, so 40 = 2x, x = 20, and the angle is 3(20) + 10 = 70. Easy, but students sometimes give x = 20 as the answer.","with":"The question asks for the angle (3x + 10), so the options are angles. Test 40 first: 3x + 10 = 40 gives x = 10, and the other angle is 5(10) - 30 = 20. They are not equal, and 20 is less than 40, so I need a bigger angle. Test 70: 3x + 10 = 70 gives x = 20, and the other angle is 5(20) - 30 = 70. They match. Option C (70°) is correct.","result":"Two tests. Back-solving also protects against the trap of answering x = 20, because I test angles, not x."},{"skill":"li","q":4,"without":"21,000 - 5,000 = 16,000, divided by 1,500 = 10.67, so the whole number is 10. Students often round to 11.","with":"The question asks for the greatest number, so I check the big options. Test 11: 5,000 + 11 x 1,500 = 5,000 + 16,500 = 21,500, which is more than 21,000. Too much. Test 10: 5,000 + 15,000 = 20,000, which is within 21,000. Since 11 fails and 10 works, the greatest is 10. Option B is correct.","result":"For 'greatest' I tested from the top and stopped at the first option that worked. I also saw why 11 fails, so I do not make the rounding mistake."},{"skill":"nf","q":8,"without":"No real solutions means the discriminant is negative: 64 - 4m < 0, so m > 16. Many students forget the rule or use 'greater than or equal to'.","with":"Sort the options: 12, 15, 16, 17. Test 15: x² - 8x + 15 = 0 gives 64 - 60 = 4, which is positive, so there are two solutions. Not allowed. Test 16: 64 - 64 = 0, so there is exactly one solution (x = 4). Still not 'no real solutions'. Test 17: 64 - 68 = -4, which is negative, so there is no real solution. Option D (17) is correct.","result":"Three tests with simple arithmetic, no rule to remember. Testing 16 shows the edge case that tricks students."}],"experiment":"Choose 8 questions whose options are numbers. Solve 4 the usual way and 4 by back-solving, starting from a middle value. Write down the time for each. Also write down how many tests you needed each time. If back-solving is slower for one-step questions, you will see it. Then you will know which question types to use it on."},{"id":"u11","name":"Pick your own numbers","applies":"m","oneline":"When a problem has letters but no numbers, I put in easy numbers of my own and test the options.","story":"When my friends and I want to know whether a plan is fair, we do not argue in the air. We say, let us pretend the bill is 100 and see what each person pays. The talk becomes easy because we use a real number. The SAT sometimes asks a question with only letters, or only percents, where nothing is real. The code is the same as with my friends: make the problem real by choosing a number that is easy to work with, like 100 or 10, and then see which option gives the same result.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["Some Math questions hide the numbers. They say 'x', or 'a certain number', or 'a store raised its price by 20 percent'. The code is to put in numbers you choose, do the calculation with them, and then put the same numbers in each option. The option that gives the same result is the answer.","This works because if an algebra statement is true for all numbers, it is true for the numbers you pick."]},{"h":"The steps","p":["1. Choose easy numbers. For percent, use 100. For a total in a ratio, use 10 or 100. For an expression with x, use x = 2 or x = 3.","2. Calculate the question with your numbers and write the target number.","3. Put the same numbers into every option.","4. The option that gives your target is the answer. Test all four options, because sometimes two options match one number.","5. If two options match, pick another number and test only those two."]},{"h":"Which numbers to avoid","p":["Do not use 0 or 1 when you are testing expressions. With 0 and 1, many different expressions give the same result, so you cannot tell which option is right. Example: with x = 0, every term that has an x disappears, so only the last number is left.","Do not pick the same number for two different letters (x = 3 and y = 3). Then an option that mixes them looks right for the wrong reason.","Do not pick a number that makes a denominator 0. Do not pick a number the question forbids, such as 'x not equal to 3'.","Choose small, different, whole numbers such as 2, 3 or 5."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Which expression is equal to 2(x + 3)? Options: 2x + 3, 2x + 6, x + 6, 2x + 5. Pick x = 2. Target: 2(5) = 10. Options give: 7, 10, 8, 9. Only 2x + 6 gives 10.","Now try the unlucky pick x = 0. Target: 6. Options give: 3, 6, 6, 5. Two match, so x = 0 would not decide. This is why we avoid 0."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Use it for questions that ask 'which expression is equivalent', for percent-change questions with no real starting amount, for ratio questions with unknown totals, and for geometry questions that compare shapes with letters such as r and h.","Do not use it when the problem already has numbers you can use, or when the question asks for the value of a letter."]}],"steps":["Choose easy numbers: 100 for percent, 10 for totals, 2 or 3 for x.","Avoid 0, 1 and equal values for different letters.","Calculate the question with your numbers and write the target.","Put the same numbers in all four options.","Pick the option that matches. If two match, try another number."],"why":"The SAT writes expression and percent questions so that they are true for every number, which means any allowed number you choose gives the right answer. Calculating with a small number such as 2 or 100 is much easier than doing algebra with letters, and four short calculations are fast. Every Math question has four options and only one is correct, so testing all four finds it. The code also gives you a way to check an answer you got from algebra.","watch":"A bad pick can make two options give the same value, and then you have to try again. Never pick 0 or 1 for expressions. Also, you must test all four options, not just the first match, and you must not pick a number that the question does not allow. The code does not work for questions that ask for a particular value, such as 'what is x'.","apply":[{"skill":"eq","q":4,"without":"Remove the brackets with care: (3x² + 5x - 2) - x² + 4x - 6. Combine: 2x² + 9x - 8. Many students forget to change the sign of 4x and 6, and choose 2x² + x - 8.","with":"Pick x = 2. Target: (12 + 10 - 2) - (4 - 8 + 6) = 20 - 2 = 18. Test the options at x = 2: 2x² + x - 8 = 2; 2x² + 9x - 8 = 18; 2x² + 9x + 4 = 30; 4x² + x + 4 = 22. Only the second option gives 18. Option B is correct. (Try the unlucky pick x = 0: the target is -8, and options A and B both give -8, so x = 0 cannot decide.)","result":"Four short calculations instead of one long algebra line, and the unlucky pick x = 0 is shown honestly as a warning."},{"skill":"eq","q":5,"without":"Factor 6x² + x - 12 by trial: try pairs of factors until the middle term is x. Often 4 to 6 attempts.","with":"Pick x = 1. Target: 6 + 1 - 12 = -5. Test the options at x = 1: (3x + 4)(2x - 3) = 7 x -1 = -7; (6x - 4)(x + 3) = 2 x 4 = 8; (3x - 4)(2x + 3) = -1 x 5 = -5; (3x - 2)(2x + 6) = 1 x 8 = 8. Only option C gives -5. Option C is correct. (The unlucky pick x = 0 would give -12 for all four options, so it is useless.)","result":"The very bad pick x = 0 is shown in the brackets: all four options equal -12 there. This is a clear example of why we avoid 0."},{"skill":"pc","q":7,"without":"Call the start S. After +20% it is 1.2S; after -25% it is 0.75 x 1.2S = 0.9S. Then 0.9S = 54,000 and S = 60,000. Needs a good idea about multiplying factors.","with":"Pick a start of 100. After +20%: 120. After -25% of 120 (which is 30): 90. So the net effect is that 100 becomes 90, a factor of 0.9. The final revenue is 54,000, so the start was 54,000 divided by 0.9 = 60,000. Test: 60,000 x 1.2 = 72,000, and 72,000 x 0.75 = 54,000. Option B is correct.","result":"The easy base 100 shows that the two percents do not cancel (120 then 90, not 100). That kills the trap of '+20% and -25% means -5%'."},{"skill":"av","q":8,"without":"Write the volume formulas with letters: V = πr²h = 40, and B = π(3r)²(h/3) = 9πr² x h/3 = 3πr²h = 3 x 40 = 120. Needs care with the squared 3.","with":"Pick r = 1 and h = 1. Cylinder A: π x 1 x 1 = π. Cylinder B: radius 3 and height 1/3, so π x 9 x (1/3) = 3π. Cylinder B is 3 times as big as A. So its volume is 3 x 40 = 120. Option C is correct. (I chose r and h as 1 here because geometry numbers like these do not break the shape. The ratio is the same for any r and h.)","result":"One tiny calculation shows the factor 3, and then I multiply by 40. The squared 3 is handled without a mistake."},{"skill":"lf","q":8,"without":"Use the rule h(x + 3) - h(x) = 12 to see that the slope is 4, then write h(x) = 4x + b, use h(1) = 2 to find b = -2, and compute h(10). About 4 steps.","with":"The rule says every 3 steps in x adds 12 to h, so h is a straight line that rises 4 per step. Choose the simplest function that fits: h(x) = 4x + b. Because h(1) = 2, 4 + b = 2, so b = -2. Then h(10) = 40 - 2 = 38. Option B is correct. Check: h(4) - h(1) = 14 - 2 = 12.","result":"I picked a simple concrete function that obeys the rule instead of working with the abstract rule. This is legal because the question says h is linear."},{"skill":"pc","q":5,"without":"Let w be the wholesale price: 1.4w x 0.85 = 119, so 1.19w = 119 and w = 100. A student who misses that 1.4 and 0.85 multiply may subtract 40% - 15% = 25%.","with":"Pick a wholesale price of 100. Mark up 40%: 140. Then take 15% off: 140 x 0.85 = 119. The selling price is exactly 119. So the wholesale price is 100. Option B is correct.","result":"The easy base of 100 hits the answer exactly, in two steps, and shows that the two percents do not simply subtract."}],"experiment":"Find 6 questions that ask about expressions, percent change or ratios. Do 3 with the usual algebra and 3 by choosing your own numbers. For each one, write down the time and if the choice of numbers worked the first time. If two options matched your number, count that as one failed pick. After that, compare the time and the failed picks."},{"id":"u12","name":"The five-second check","applies":"all","oneline":"Before I lock any answer, I spend five seconds to check four things, and I save marks I already earned.","story":"Before I leave the house, I always pat my pockets: phone, keys, money. It takes three seconds. On the days I skip it, I remember at the bus stop that I left my phone on the table and the whole morning is worse. A lock-in on the SAT is the same. After working for a minute and a half, I pat my pockets one last time: What was asked? Which unit? Which sign? Is my answer really one of the options as written? It costs five seconds and it protects the minute and a half I already spent.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["The five-second check is a short habit that you do after you have chosen an answer and before you press the next button. You check four things in order.","1. What was asked? Look at the last sentence once more. Is my answer the thing it asks for?","2. Units. Is my number in minutes, meters, dollars or hours as the question wants?","3. The sign. Is it plus or minus, bigger or smaller, greater than or less than? Did I flip the inequality when I divided by a negative?","4. Is my answer in the options, as written? If I got 0.5 and the options say 1/2, fine. If I got 6 and no option says 6, something is wrong."]},{"h":"Why these four","p":["These are the four places where good students lose marks they have already earned. The student did the hard maths, and then answered the wrong question, forgot to change the unit, lost a minus sign or picked an option that looks the same as the answer but is not.","In Reading and Writing the check is the same idea: the last sentence tells you what to look for (the main idea, a word that means the same, the best transition), and a quick re-read stops you from picking an option that answers a different question."]},{"h":"The steps","p":["1. Re-read the last sentence of the question. Not the whole question, only the last sentence.","2. Look at the units in the question and in your answer.","3. Test the sign with one easy value (for example, put a number into the inequality).","4. Put your finger on the option you chose and read it once, in full.","5. Lock it."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Question: A runner runs 5 kilometers at 12 kilometers per hour. How many minutes? You got 5 / 12 = 0.4167. Check 1: the question asks for minutes. Check 2: 0.4167 is in hours. Convert: 0.4167 x 60 = 25. The answer is 25 minutes. The check saved the question.","Do this drill with 3 questions today and time yourself. It should take less than 5 seconds."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["Always, but only after you have an answer. Do not use the check as an excuse to start the whole problem again. It is a quick last look. If something looks wrong, fix it. If not, move on."]}],"steps":["Re-read only the last sentence of the question.","Check the unit of your answer against the unit asked.","Check the sign with one easy value.","Find your answer in the options as written and read that option in full.","Lock it and move on."],"why":"Many of the wrong options on the SAT are placed there on purpose, to catch students who answered in the wrong unit, with the wrong sign or for the wrong target. Because the score depends only on the number of correct answers and a wrong answer is not penalised, every mark you protect is a mark gained. Each Math question gives you about 95 seconds and each Reading and Writing question about 71 seconds, so a five-second check is a small part of the time. This applies to every question, because every question has a last sentence, a type of answer and four options.","watch":"Do not let the check turn into a full second solution. If you cannot find the problem in five seconds, trust your first work and move on, or mark it for review and return. Also the check can only find mistakes in the final step. It does not repair a wrong plan.","apply":[{"skill":"rr","q":6,"without":"A student calculates 5 / 12 = 0.4167 hours, sees no option for it and looks for the closest. Some pick 2.4 because 12 / 5 = 2.4, which is the speed divided the wrong way. That is a lost question.","with":"The last sentence asks for MINUTES. My answer, 0.4167, is in hours. Check 2 (units) sends me to convert: 0.4167 x 60 = 25. Check 4: 25 is in the options. Option A (25) is correct. The option 2.4 is the trap from dividing the wrong way round.","result":"Five seconds spotted that the unit was hours, not minutes, and saved a question that the maths had already earned."},{"skill":"rt","q":7,"without":"The ladder starts with its bottom at sqrt(625 - 576) = 7 feet. After the top slides to 20 feet, the bottom is at sqrt(625 - 400) = 15 feet. Students stop at 15 and choose it, because it is the last thing they calculated.","with":"I re-read the last sentence: 'How many feet does the bottom of the ladder move away from the wall?' That is a change, not the final position. Check 1 tells me to subtract: 15 - 7 = 8. Option C (8) is correct, and 15 (option D) is the trap.","result":"One re-read turned the final position (15) into the movement (8). The target was a difference, not a position."},{"skill":"li","q":3,"without":"Subtract 4: -3x ≥ 12. Divide by -3 and forget to flip: x ≥ -4. That is option B, a classic mistake.","with":"I finish with x ≤ -4 after flipping. The sign check: put in x = -5 (which is ≤ -4): -3(-5) + 4 = 19, and 19 ≥ 16 is true. Put in x = -3 (which is ≥ -4 but not ≤ -4): -3(-3) + 4 = 13, and 13 ≥ 16 is false. So the answer must be x ≤ -4. Option A is correct.","result":"One easy test number proves the direction of the sign. Five seconds, and option B is exposed."},{"skill":"unit","q":3,"without":"A student divides 150 by 2.54 and gets 59.055, then wonders which option to choose and may pick 59.1, or might multiply by mistake and pick 381.0.","with":"Check 1: the last sentence asks for inches, to the nearest tenth. Check 2: centimeters to inches means dividing by 2.54, and the answer must be smaller than 150, which kills 381.0 and 152.5. Check 4: 59.055 rounded to the nearest tenth is 59.1, and that is written in the options. Option A (59.1) is correct.","result":"Two options (381.0 and 152.5) were removed by a size check in a second, and the rounding was matched to the option as written."},{"skill":"wic","q":4,"without":"A student sees 'grave' and picks 'solemn' because it is the first meaning that comes to mind in a sad or serious setting. The word is placed in a news sentence about money and the library closing.","with":"Check 1: the question asks what 'grave' means AS USED IN THE TEXT. The text says 'grave financial situation, warning that without new income the library and the clinic would both close.' The situation is very bad, with big results. 'Serious' fits. 'Solemn' describes a mood or a person's manner, not a financial situation. Option B (serious) is correct.","result":"The phrase 'as used in the text' sends me back to the sentence instead of to my usual meaning of the word. This is the same check in Reading and Writing."},{"skill":"ce","q":8,"without":"A student sees that School P had 120 supporters and School Q had 65, thinks P looks bigger, and picks option A, or picks the numbers that look strong.","with":"Check 1: the claim says 'relatively more popular at School Q than at School P'. Relative means a share (percent), not a count. The schools have different sizes: 200 and 100. Option D gives the percents: 65 of 100 is 65 percent and 120 of 200 is 60 percent. 65 percent is more than 60 percent, so the claim is supported. Option D is correct.","result":"Reading the word 'relatively' again in the claim changes the question from 'who has more' to 'who has the bigger share'. Option A answers the wrong question."}],"experiment":"For one week, do the five-second check on every question you do in this app. Keep a tally: each time the check made you change your answer, write one mark. After 40 questions, count the marks. If it saved you a few questions, you have your proof. If it saved you nothing, you have a good solid way to know you already check well."},{"id":"u13","name":"When two are correct, pick the shorter","applies":"rw","oneline":"If two options are both correct in grammar and in meaning, the test prefers the one with fewer words, and I know exactly when that is true and when it is a trap.","story":"When I give directions to a visitor in Kigali, I do not say, 'Go forward on the road in front of you until you reach the point where the road turns to the left side.' I say, 'Go straight, then turn left.' Both are correct, but the short one is better because the visitor does not get lost in the words. The SAT thinks the same way. When two sentences say exactly the same thing correctly, the plain short one is the one the test wants. The danger is when a short sentence leaves something out.","teach":[{"h":"What it is","p":["In some Reading and Writing questions, two options are both free of grammar mistakes and both say the same thing. One has more words than the other. In that case, the SAT prefers the one that is clear and concise: it has no repeated ideas and no extra words that add nothing.","The code: first make sure each option is correct, then if two are left, pick the shorter one."]},{"h":"The steps","p":["1. Read what the question asks. For Conventions it is 'conforms to the conventions of Standard English'. For Rhetorical Synthesis it is the goal in the last sentence.","2. Remove every option that has a grammar mistake or that does not do the goal (leaves out something the goal needs).","3. Count what is left. If one option is left, choose it. Stop.","4. If exactly two options are left and they say the same thing, count the words and choose the shorter one.","5. If the shorter one lost an idea the goal needs, it was not a tie. Go back to step 2."]},{"h":"What 'the same thing' means","p":["Two options are a true tie only when both are free of mistakes and both give the same information. Example: 'The reason he was late was because of the rain' and 'He was late because of the rain'. Both say one idea. The second is shorter and cleaner, so the second wins.","But 'The lamp costs more' and 'The lamp costs more at first but saves money later' are not a tie. The second gives more information. If the goal needs the savings, the longer one is the answer."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Which is better? a) 'Every student has to bring a book to class, and the book must be brought by each student.' b) 'Every student has to bring a book to class.' Both are correct, but (a) says the same thing twice, and (b) is shorter. Choose (b).","Now try: a) 'She studies.' b) 'She studies hard every night to pass the exam.' The goal is to show how she prepares. The short one lacks the information. Here the long one wins."]},{"h":"How to know when to use it","p":["It fires in questions where the options are different ways to write the same idea, especially where one is wordy: repeated words, 'in order to' versus 'to', 'the reason is because', passive forms that add words.","It does not fire when the options differ in punctuation only, or in a single word such as a transition. Then the length is the same and meaning or grammar decides."]}],"steps":["Remove options with grammar mistakes or that miss the goal.","See how many are left. If one, choose it.","If two are left and both say the same thing, count the words.","Choose the shorter one.","If the shorter one lost an idea the goal needs, it was not a tie, so go back."],"why":"Reading and Writing questions are built around clear, direct writing. When two options are both correct and equal in meaning, the extra words in the longer one can only add repetition or clutter, so the plainer one is the better writing. The code is safe only after you remove every option with a mistake or a missing idea, because the test also has many questions in which the longest option is correct since it gives needed information. The order matters: correctness first, goal second, length last.","watch":"This code is a tie-breaker, never a first step. In Rhetorical Synthesis the right answer is often the longest one, because the goal asks for two things (a benefit and a risk, a finding and its limit). In Boundaries and Form, Structure and Sense, the options often have the same number of words. If you pick the shortest option without checking the goal and the grammar, you will fall into the trap.","apply":[{"skill":"rs","q":2,"without":"A student likes short, simple sentences and picks option C ('Kerosene lamps need fuel, and solar lamps do not'), 9 words, the shortest.","with":"Step 1: the goal is to explain why a solar lamp can be cheaper over time even though it costs more at first. Step 2: option C has no price and no 'at first', so it does not show 'costs more at first'. It fails the goal. Option D (12 words) says nothing about cost. Option A (17 words) gives the higher price but not the running cost. Only option B (30 words) has both the higher price and the monthly fuel cost. It is the longest, and it is correct.","result":"Here the code does NOT fire, because the shorter options lose information the goal needs. This is the honest trap: the longest option is the answer. Option B is correct."},{"skill":"rs","q":8,"without":"A student sees a long first option (31 words) and thinks 'too wordy', so picks option B (14 words): 'Okoye studied traders in three cities and found that mobile payments come with fees.'","with":"Step 1: the goal is to summarise Okoye's conclusion while acknowledging a downside. Step 2: option B has the downside (fees) but not the conclusion (fees were small compared with the losses avoided). It fails. Option C (13 words) says 'free of cost' and 'prevent all theft', which is false. Option D (16 words) says 'no downsides', which ignores the fees. Only option A (31 words) has the conclusion and the downside. It is the longest, and it is correct.","result":"One option survives the goal check, so length is never used. The code correctly stays silent."},{"skill":"fss","q":5,"without":"A student looks at options with 6, 6, 10 and 6 words and tries to use the shortest-wins rule, but there is no clear shortest.","with":"Step 1: the text begins 'Walking along the shore at sunset,' so the person walking must be the next subject. Step 2: option B ('Chantal watched the waves turn gold') has Chantal as the walker. The other options start with the waves, a golden light or the sunset, which cannot walk. Only B survives. Three options have 6 words and one has 10, so length is no help anyway. Option B is correct.","result":"Grammar decides alone. Three options have the same length, so the code has nothing to say, and that is the right result."},{"skill":"bd","q":1,"without":"A student sees the options 'football my sister' (3 words), 'football, my sister' (3 words), 'football, and my sister' (4 words) and 'football; and my sister' (4 words), and picks a short one.","with":"Step 2: 'football my sister' has no punctuation and runs two sentences together. 'football, my sister' is a comma splice. The semicolon option 'football; and my sister' is not the standard pattern. The comma plus 'and' joins two full sentences in Standard English. Option C is correct. It is one of the longest options.","result":"The correct option is longer than the two shortest. Grammar always comes before length."},{"skill":"tr","q":3,"without":"A student tries to use the code on a transition question and counts words: 1, 2, 1 and 1.","with":"The options are 'moreover,' 'for example,' 'similarly,' and 'however,'. The text says many students believe memorizing all night is best and research suggests short sessions over several days work better. The ideas are opposite, so I need a contrast word. Option D ('however,') is correct. The length of a transition tells nothing about it.","result":"The code correctly stays silent for transitions. The relationship between the two sentences decides."}],"experiment":"Choose 10 Reading and Writing questions from Rhetorical Synthesis and the Standard English skills. For each, first remove the wrong options. Then write if you ended with one option or two, and in the two-option cases, which was shorter and if it was the key answer. Count how often the shorter one was right. Compare your count with a friend's, and see if the rule held in your questions."},{"id":"u14","name":"Use every Bluebook tool","applies":"all","oneline":"The Bluebook app gives me tools that paper never did, and I learn where each one helps so that I use them without thinking.","story":"A good cook does not use one knife for everything. Rice needs a big spoon, onions need a sharp knife, and the pan has a lid for a reason. A student who uses only the pen is like a cook with one spoon. Bluebook gives you a toolbox: highlights, a line reader, a way to cross out options, a bookmark, a menu of questions, a formula sheet and zoom. Each saves time or stops a mistake. I want you to know the toolbox before test day.","teach":[{"h":"The toolbox in one list","p":["These are the tools on the Bluebook test screen: the testing timer (you can hide it until 5 minutes remain), the Desmos graphing calculator (for all Math questions), the Math reference sheet, highlights and notes, mark for review (a bookmark), the line reader, the option eliminator (cross out choices, and undo them), the question menu (jump to any question in the module), and zoom.","You can move back and forth inside a module until its time ends, but once you move on from a module you cannot go back. Plan your tools with that in mind."]},{"h":"Reading and Writing tools","p":["Highlights and notes. Highlight the clue words in a passage: the 'but', the 'however', the claim, the number. Use a note for a short reminder, for example 'T1: rain now. T2: rain before'. It stops you losing the point when you read the options.","Line reader. It helps you keep your place in a long passage and read one part at a time. Use it when your eyes jump or when the text is long.","Option eliminator. Cross out options you are sure are wrong. It makes the choice smaller: from four to three to two. You can undo it, so nothing is lost if you change your mind.","Zoom. Make the text or the table bigger if your eyes are tired, especially on a small screen."]},{"h":"Math tools","p":["Reference sheet. It shows geometry facts and formulas, for example the area of a circle and the volume of a cone. Look at it for the formula instead of trying to remember it. Do not waste time learning what is already on the page.","Desmos. For systems, curves and regression. See the code 'Desmos first' for the exact moves.","Zoom. Use it on tables and graphs, to read each number clearly."]},{"h":"Moving through the module","p":["Mark for review. If a question is taking too long, choose your best guess (there is no penalty for a wrong answer, so never leave a blank), mark it, and move on.","Question menu. It shows all the questions in the module. At the end, open it to see which are unanswered and which you marked, and jump straight to them. Use your remaining minutes for the marked ones.","Timer. Hiding it until 5 minutes remain can remove panic. Learn if that works for you in practice."]},{"h":"A tiny drill","p":["Open a practice test in Bluebook (it is free from the College Board). Spend 10 minutes only learning the tools: highlight a sentence, add a note, cross out an option and undo it, mark a question, open the question menu, open the reference sheet, and zoom in and out. Do this until nothing surprises you."]}],"steps":["Highlight the clue or claim in the passage, and add a short note if you need one.","Use the line reader on a long passage and zoom on tables or small text.","Cross out wrong options with the eliminator as you go.","In Math, check the reference sheet for a formula and use Desmos for graphs.","Mark any question you guessed on, and use the question menu at the end to return to it."],"why":"The Digital SAT is taken on the Bluebook app, and College Board includes these tools because they are part of the test, so every student has them. Each tool saves a small amount of time or stops a small mistake: a highlight keeps the clue in view, the eliminator makes a four-way choice smaller, the reference sheet removes a formula you might forget and the question menu lets you return to a marked question. Each module is timed and you can go back only inside the module you are in, so using the menu and marks before time ends is a real advantage.","watch":"A tool is only useful if you have practised it. If you use a tool for the first time on test day, it can cost time. Also, do not highlight everything, because then nothing stands out, and do not cross out an option unless you can say why it is wrong. Remember that once you leave a module you cannot return to it.","apply":[{"skill":"ctc","q":8,"without":"A student reads Text 1, then Text 2, then looks at four options and has to remember what each text said.","with":"Tool: Highlights and notes. Highlight in Text 1: 'sense distant rain' and 'travel towards the storms'. Highlight in Text 2: 'towards areas where rain fell several days earlier, not towards areas where rain is falling'. Add a note: 'T1: toward rain NOW. T2: toward rain BEFORE.' Now the question asks what Text 2 most directly challenges: the claim that wildebeest sense distant rain and move towards it. Option C is correct.","result":"Two highlights and one note kept both claims on the screen and made the difference clear, so I did not need to scroll back."},{"skill":"tsp","q":7,"without":"A student reads the four-sentence science passage, loses the place after 'Then, in 1977,' and has to read the passage again to describe its structure.","with":"Tool: Line reader. Turn on the line reader and read sentence by sentence. Sentence 1: the assumption (too hot for life). Sentence 2: the 1977 finding (life found). Sentence 3: how the animals live (bacteria turn chemicals into food). Sentence 4: a wider possibility (life on other worlds). Option C matches: assumption, finding that overturned it, how it works, wider possibility.","result":"One careful read with the line reader gave me four steps in order, so I did not read the passage twice."},{"skill":"wic","q":8,"without":"A student keeps all four words in mind and compares them with the sentence several times.","with":"Tool: Option eliminator. The text says the recovery 'may be ______' because growth returned at the surface but the roots remain badly weakened. Cross out 'gradual' (nothing says slow), 'unprecedented' (nothing says it never happened before) and 'substantial' (the roots are weak). Only 'illusory' (not real) is left. Option A is correct.","result":"Three options crossed out in one pass, and I can undo each of them if I change my mind."},{"skill":"inf2","q":7,"without":"A student spends three minutes on the sample-size problem, runs short of time and rushes the last questions.","with":"Tool: Mark for review and question menu. Read the problem. If the idea is not clear in about 20 seconds, pick a guess first (there is no penalty), press mark for review and move on. At the end of the module, open the question menu, see the marked question and go back. Now I use the idea that to cut the margin of error from 6 to 1.5 (4 times smaller) the sample must be 4 x 4 = 16 times bigger: 50 x 16 = 800. Option B (800) is correct.","result":"The question gets an answer immediately, and I come back to it with spare time. Easy questions are not rushed."},{"skill":"av","q":6,"without":"A student cannot remember if the cone has a one-third in front, and may calculate 6 x 6 x 8 x π = 288π (option B).","with":"Tool: Reference sheet. Open the reference sheet and find the cone: V = (1/3)πr²h. Put in r = 6 and h = 8: (1/3) x π x 36 x 8 = 96π. Option A (96π) is correct, and the 288π (option B) is exposed as the answer without the one-third.","result":"One look at the sheet gives the formula for sure, and a tempting option is removed."},{"skill":"pr","q":3,"without":"A student squints at the small numbers in the two-way table and reads the wrong row or column and uses the wrong numbers.","with":"Tool: Zoom. Zoom in on the table. The question is about students who travel by bus: that row has 25 primary, 45 secondary, 70 total. The probability that one of them is in secondary is 45/70 = 9/14. Option B is correct.","result":"A bigger table made the row easy to read and the right numbers (45 and 70) easy to take."}],"experiment":"Take two practice sets of 10 questions. In the first set, use only your pen and the mouse. In the second set, use the tools in this code: highlights, the eliminator, mark for review, the reference sheet and zoom. Note the time, the number of correct answers and how many questions you were able to return to. Compare them, and keep the tools that helped you."}];